1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,580
Two friends order the
same lunch on Kavya's app.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:06,832
Rohan lives one point
nine kilometres away.

3
00:00:06,832 --> 00:00:08,120
He pays nothing.

4
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:11,424
Fatima lives two point
one kilometres away.

5
00:00:11,424 --> 00:00:13,055
She pays thirty rupees.

6
00:00:13,055 --> 00:00:13,955
Fair?

7
00:00:14,211 --> 00:00:15,482
Meet Dabba Dash.

8
00:00:15,482 --> 00:00:20,421
Kavya, a student in Pune, built it
to deliver lunch around her college.

9
00:00:20,721 --> 00:00:23,426
The food comes from
Joseph uncle's canteen.

10
00:00:23,426 --> 00:00:26,980
Rohan runs the servers, and
Fatima checks every number.

11
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:32,156
The delivery fee is a function: a rule
where each input gives exactly one

12
00:00:32,156 --> 00:00:33,056
output.

13
00:00:33,114 --> 00:00:36,460
Here, the input is distance,
and the output is the fee.

14
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,163
All the allowed inputs are the domain.

15
00:00:39,163 --> 00:00:41,612
All the possible outputs are the range.

16
00:00:42,162 --> 00:00:44,253
The trouble is at two kilometres.

17
00:00:44,253 --> 00:00:46,114
To see it, we need limits.

18
00:00:46,414 --> 00:00:51,734
In 1821, a French mathematician
wrote down the idea.

19
00:00:52,034 --> 00:00:56,375
When values come closer and closer
to a fixed value, that value is their

20
00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:57,275
limit.

21
00:00:57,252 --> 00:01:01,554
Simply put, a limit is where a function
is heading as the input gets close

22
00:01:01,554 --> 00:01:02,454
to a point.

23
00:01:02,991 --> 00:01:05,900
Watch the fee near two
kilometres, from below.

24
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,287
One point nine, one point nine
nine, one point nine nine nine.

25
00:01:10,287 --> 00:01:11,867
The fee stays at zero.

26
00:01:12,167 --> 00:01:13,799
The left limit is zero.

27
00:01:14,099 --> 00:01:15,276
Now from above.

28
00:01:15,276 --> 00:01:19,388
Two point one, two point zero
one, two point zero zero one.

29
00:01:19,688 --> 00:01:21,285
The fee stays at thirty.

30
00:01:21,285 --> 00:01:22,973
The right limit is thirty.

31
00:01:23,273 --> 00:01:26,715
The two sides disagree, so
the limit does not exist.

32
00:01:26,715 --> 00:01:27,917
That is a jump.

33
00:01:28,467 --> 00:01:31,199
Professor Iyer shows something stranger.

34
00:01:31,199 --> 00:01:34,123
x squared minus one, over x minus one.

35
00:01:34,123 --> 00:01:37,273
At x equals one, it gives zero over zero.

36
00:01:37,573 --> 00:01:38,799
No value there.

37
00:01:38,799 --> 00:01:40,451
A program would crash.

38
00:01:40,451 --> 00:01:41,729
But look nearby.

39
00:01:42,029 --> 00:01:45,664
At zero point nine nine, it
gives one point nine nine.

40
00:01:45,664 --> 00:01:48,594
At one point zero one, two point zero one.

41
00:01:48,894 --> 00:01:50,384
Both sides head to two.

42
00:01:50,384 --> 00:01:53,629
The limit is two, though the
function never gets there.

43
00:01:54,178 --> 00:01:57,226
A limit is about where you are
heading, not where you are.

44
00:01:57,876 --> 00:02:01,626
A function is continuous at a
point if it passes three checks.

45
00:02:01,926 --> 00:02:02,826
One.

46
00:02:02,431 --> 00:02:04,015
It has a value there.

47
00:02:04,315 --> 00:02:05,215
Two.

48
00:02:04,859 --> 00:02:06,226
The limit exists.

49
00:02:06,226 --> 00:02:07,870
Left and right agree.

50
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:09,070
Three.

51
00:02:08,775 --> 00:02:10,659
The limit equals the value.

52
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:14,183
So you can draw through that
point without lifting your pen.

53
00:02:14,733 --> 00:02:16,489
So Kavya fixes the fee.

54
00:02:16,489 --> 00:02:20,723
The first kilometre is free, then
fifteen rupees per kilometre.

55
00:02:21,023 --> 00:02:24,703
At two kilometres, it is fifteen
from the left, from the right, and at

56
00:02:24,703 --> 00:02:25,603
the point.

57
00:02:25,686 --> 00:02:27,449
All three checks pass.

58
00:02:27,449 --> 00:02:31,252
Rohan pays thirteen fifty,
and Fatima sixteen fifty.

59
00:02:31,252 --> 00:02:32,152
No jump.

60
00:02:32,655 --> 00:02:34,687
Where is this used at work today?

61
00:02:34,987 --> 00:02:35,887
Functions.

62
00:02:35,827 --> 00:02:39,403
A bank turns a loan amount
into one monthly payment.

63
00:02:39,703 --> 00:02:40,603
Limits.

64
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:45,146
In a hospital, a steady drip brings a
medicine's level in the blood closer

65
00:02:45,146 --> 00:02:47,364
and closer to one steady value.

66
00:02:47,664 --> 00:02:48,609
Continuity.

67
00:02:48,609 --> 00:02:53,929
Tax slabs charge a higher rate only
above each limit, so tax never jumps.

68
00:02:54,479 --> 00:02:57,194
But is the real world always continuous?

69
00:02:57,494 --> 00:02:58,616
The good side.

70
00:02:58,616 --> 00:03:02,388
Continuous rules are fair,
and calculus is built on them.

71
00:03:02,688 --> 00:03:03,907
The hard side.

72
00:03:03,907 --> 00:03:08,394
Real life moves in steps, like
whole orders and rounded prices.

73
00:03:08,694 --> 00:03:11,242
And no jumps is not smooth.

74
00:03:11,242 --> 00:03:18,042
In 1872, a German mathematician found a
curve with no breaks, jagged everywhere.

75
00:03:18,342 --> 00:03:22,451
So Kavya rounds the fees up, to
fourteen and seventeen rupees.

76
00:03:22,751 --> 00:03:25,751
Tiny one rupee steps that nobody minds.

77
00:03:26,051 --> 00:03:26,952
The lesson?

78
00:03:26,952 --> 00:03:30,480
Use smooth rules, but know
where life moves in steps.

79
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:32,047
Quick check.

80
00:03:32,047 --> 00:03:36,353
The fee is zero just below two
kilometres, and thirty just above.

81
00:03:36,353 --> 00:03:37,987
Is it continuous there?

82
00:03:39,887 --> 00:03:40,787
No!

83
00:03:40,301 --> 00:03:43,904
The left and right limits are
different, so the graph jumps.

84
00:03:44,454 --> 00:03:45,464
So, remember.

85
00:03:45,764 --> 00:03:48,971
A function gives exactly
one output for each input.

86
00:03:49,271 --> 00:03:52,505
A limit is where a function
is heading, from both sides.

87
00:03:52,805 --> 00:03:56,559
A function is continuous when
its value and its limit match.

88
00:03:57,109 --> 00:04:00,090
Next time, we'll explore
sequences and series.
